  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "To have: «near me there is»",
     "texto": [
      "To express everyday possession, you usually use **у меня есть**: «near me there is». **У меня есть брат** = I have a brother.",
      "**У тебя есть…?** (do you have…?), **у вас есть…?** (do you have…? formal), **у него есть** (he has), **у неё есть** (she has)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Not having",
     "texto": [
      "In the negative, **нет** is used instead of **есть**, and what is missing goes in the **genitive**: **У меня нет брата** (I don't have a brother), **у меня нет сестры** (I don't have a sister), **у меня нет книги** (I don't have a book).",
      "In the masculine singular, a common ending is **-а/-я**; in the feminine, **-ы/-и**. The plural has more than one pattern: **нет книг**. Learn the common forms in context."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "My, your, his/her",
     "texto": [
      "«My» and «your» agree with the thing possessed, like an adjective.",
      "As possessives, **его** (his) and **её** (her) do not change according to the gender or number of the thing possessed: **его брат / его сестра / его родители**. In **Его зовут Сергей**, **его** means «to him». **Его** is pronounced «yih-VOH»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
